<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mengu-Timur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1280</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Möngke Temür</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mengu-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> from 1266 or 1267 until 1280, a grandson of Batu. He struck coins in his own name, marking the Horde's effective independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>, and granted trading privileges to the Genoese at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a>. He continued the alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and issued charters exempting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> from tribute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mengu-timur.jpg" alt="Mengu-Timur" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1405-1409 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mengu-Timur_portrait._Timurid_genealogy,_1405-1409,_Samarkand_(Topkapi_Sarayi_M%C3%BCzesi,_H2152)_folio_36b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
